osed, but the operational intent should be documented at implementation."},{"action":"present","contentHash":"sha256:350b71561fa1045b4fe59df294459d7ca56450960b8b3dc451f2c68ce599840f","evidenceId":"shadow-vote-assessment:sha256:a2771a623b3c49656c1a5cae8c2681420c1479d16889cac28e132c0b59085abd","kind":"ARTIFACT","path":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v5/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-google-350b71561fa1045b4fe59df294459d7ca56450960b8b3dc451f2c68ce599840f.json","producedBy":{"role":"REVIEWER","threadId":null,"tool":"shadow-council-harness"},"sourcePath":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v5/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-google-350b71561fa1045b4fe59df294459d7ca56450960b8b3dc451f2c68ce599840f.json","summary":"The proposal resolves both prior blocking deficiencies by enforcing safe-integer bounds (0 <= stopEpoch <= 2^53-1) with fail-closed exhaustion in the canonical serializer and schema, while establishing kernel SO_PEERCRED exact-UID authorization against a root-owned policy with strictly zero V1C execution consumers. Residual caveat: runtime deployment must strictly enforce host file permissions on the root-owned UID policy before daemon initialization."},{"action":"present","contentHash":"sha256:31bc75781c76d0bf1748e58d47bd75d126e055966fb77fada37ed73d404a04f2","evidenceId":"shadow-vote-assessment:sha256:e2647c0a9ce1e2e0b456eb91fc494d153d5631322aad13074fec852ac373d46f","kind":"ARTIFACT","path":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v5/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-openai-31bc75781c76d0bf1748e58d47bd75d126e055966fb77fada37ed73d404a04f2.json","producedBy":{"role":"REVIEWER","threadId":null,"tool":"shadow-council-harness"},"sourcePath":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v5/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-openai-31bc75781c76d0bf1748e58d47bd75d126e055966fb77fada37ed73d404a04f2.json","summary":"The candidate materially fixes both prior blockers: stopEpoch is losslessly bounded to the frozen JSON/JS safe-integer domain with enforced exhaustion behavior, and every privileged RPC uses kernel-authenticated UID authorization against a root-owned, fail-closed role allowlist rather than peer groups. It also establishes mechanically enforceable ADMIN/CLIENT separation and leaves grant consumption entirely dormant, with no V1C execution-effect route."}],"evidenceManifestHash":"sha256:c3f1f39152c142dffea28b6069613336593f66e487e43ff1cbe0cb7a75282bc8"}
